course: dictionary
lesson: 9362
title: Who is speaking for them?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A spokesperson is someone whose job is to speak for a group, such as a company, a government or an organisation. They give the news and answer questions. «A spokesperson speaks officially for a group», so when you hear one, you are hearing the group's official view.
explain: Read the words around the word. «a police spokesperson said ...» tells you the police, as a group, are speaking through this person. Ask yourself: whose words are these, and who gave them permission to speak?
explain: Compare a spokesperson with an anonymous source. A source gives information but does not want their name used, like a «source close to the team». A reporter should check what that source says carefully, because nobody can say who they are.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. Does «the person who speaks for the group» fit in the sentence? If it does, you have understood the word.
fact: A spokesperson is someone who speaks for a group, company, government or organisation.
fact: A spokesperson gives news and answers questions for the group they work for.
fact: What a spokesperson says is the official view of the group, not just their own opinion.
fact: Plurals of «spokesperson» are spokespersons or spokespeople. Older words are «spokesman» and «spokeswoman».
fact: A named spokesperson and an unnamed source are different: the spokesperson speaks openly for a group, while an anonymous source may stay hidden.
exercise: «A police spokesperson said the road would be closed.» Who is the spokesperson here? | The road that is closed | A reporter who writes the news | A police officer who drives the car | *Someone who speaks for the police
exercise: What is the main job of a spokesperson? | Driving a bus for the group | Writing poems alone at home | Keeping every fact secret from the public | *Speaking for a group and answering questions
exercise: Which word is the plural of spokesperson? | *spokespeople | spokespersons's | speakers | spokespeoples
test: «A government spokesperson said the new plans were ready.» What does this tell you? | Only the spokesperson's private opinion, with no link to the government | The view of a school cook | *The government's official view | A view from a hidden source who will not be named
test: Why should you know who a spokesperson works for? | It stops the news being shared | *It helps you think about whose side they are giving | It tells you how tall they are | It means the news can never be wrong
exam: What is the difference between a spokesperson and an anonymous source? | A spokesperson is always anonymous, and a source is always named | They are two names for the same person | *A spokesperson speaks officially for a group, while an anonymous source does not want their name used | A source works for the news, and a spokesperson works for the police
